# `getattr(<unit alias>, "<literal>", <default>)` answered at compile time.
#
# A unit is a compile-time namespace, not a value, so the receiver died inside
# ParseExpr as `undefined variable (backend)` BEFORE the getattr arm's own logic
# ran -- the fold therefore has to sit above that ParseExpr, not inside the
# field logic below it. Receiver, name and miss are all decidable there: the
# unit is known, the name is a literal, and UnitDeclaresNameExactly answers
# without erroring. No runtime module object is involved.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH A FIXTURE. This is the construct lekkerzeilen's platform seam
# uses to ask whether the selected backend can open audio and a controller, and
# a capability probe is exactly the shape that must keep working when the answer
# FLIPS: `_pxx.py` does not define those names today and
# task-b-write-the-lekkerzeilen-pxx-platform-backend is going to add them. So the
# absent arm and the present arm are both live rows, not one row and one
# hypothetical, and a fixture covering only today's answer would go quietly
# wrong the day that ticket lands.
# bug-n-a-module-bound-by-an-import-is-not-a-value
#
# NOT COVERED, ON PURPOSE, and said here so it is not read as an oversight: a
# member the unit declares as neither a proc nor a symbol -- a CLASS -- still
# reports `undefined variable`, where CPython answers the class. Folding it from
# a third route would be the second path that stays broken
# (devdocs/dev/normalise-dont-special-case.md); it belongs in the qualifier
# door. Residual recorded on the ticket above.
import nilpy_unitgetattr as u

print("g_absent", u.g_absent())
print("g_proc", u.g_proc())
print("g_sym", u.g_sym())
print("g_sym_arith", u.g_sym_arith())
print("g_dflt", u.g_dflt())
print("h_absent", u.h_absent())
print("h_proc", u.h_proc())
print("h_sym", u.h_sym())
print("local_wins", u.local_wins())
